<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    The only project in the solution built with the Web SDK; Domain, Application and
    Infrastructure use plain Microsoft.NET.Sdk.

    CONTAINER ENTRYPOINT CONTRACT - do not add an AssemblyName property here. That property,
    and that property alone, names the emitted assembly; the SDK defaults it to this file's
    name, which is what makes the published assembly DnnMigration.Api.dll, the exact name
    docker/api.Dockerfile starts. Declaring it adds a second place the name can drift from,
    and no compiler catches the mismatch. The same path is spelled in backend/DnnMigration.sln
    and in the integration test project's project reference, and the Alpine build stage is
    case-sensitive, so the casing must not change either.

    Build policy is inherited from backend/Directory.Build.props, which is why this file
    declares no PropertyGroup. Any diagnostic that survives a Release build with warnings
    promoted to errors is fixed in code, never suppressed here.

    The API image runs as an unprivileged user and listens on 8080, so nothing added here may
    require root, a port below 1024, or a writable path outside the application directory.
    Secrets arrive as environment variables, never from a project-scoped secret store.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Swashbuckle.AspNetCore" Version="6.9.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Asp.Versioning.Mvc" Version="8.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Asp.Versioning.Mvc.ApiExplorer" Version="8.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.AspNetCore" Version="8.0.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Two references, and no more. Application supplies the service abstractions, DTOs and
    options the controllers bind to. Infrastructure is referenced so that the composition root
    this project must declare can register the persistence and security services, among them
    the database health check served at /health, which has to remain anonymous because the
    frontend container's start-up waits on it.

    Domain is deliberately not named: it arrives transitively, and listing it would advertise
    that the API may consume domain entities directly instead of crossing the boundary through
    DTOs.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Application\DnnMigration.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Infrastructure\DnnMigration.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ONE grant, for ONE question a public surface cannot answer. ApiResults.MapStatusCode returns 400 both
    for a code declared as 400 and for a code nobody declared, so no comparison of its return value can tell
    a registered failure code from a missing one. ApiResults.IsClassified answers exactly that, and it is
    internal because it is a statement about this table's completeness rather than part of the HTTP contract
    a caller consumes.

    ⚠ THE GRANT NAMES DnnMigration.IntegrationTests, AND ONLY THAT ASSEMBLY, matching the single grant
    Infrastructure already makes. The unit project has one edge, to Application, so it cannot name a type
    here in any case. Do not add a second name, do not grant to a wildcard, and do not widen a member from
    internal to public to make a test compile.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DnnMigration.IntegrationTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    DEBUG SYMBOLS ARE BUILT AND ARE NOT PUBLISHED, which is why this is a publish-time filter rather than
    <DebugType>none</DebugType>.

    What shipped before: the production image carried DnnMigration.{Api,Application,Domain,Infrastructure}.pdb
    - four portable symbol files, each embedding the absolute source paths of the machine that built them.
    Nothing in the running application reads them; the exception handler answers RFC 7807 documents that carry
    no stack trace at all, so their only effect in production is to describe the build tree to anyone who
    obtains a layer.

    Why not turn symbols off outright: the whole solution builds in Release for the test gates, and a Release
    build with no symbols reports failures without file or line numbers, which makes a red test measurably
    harder to diagnose. Filtering at publish keeps both properties - symbols where they are read, none where
    they are only exposure.

    ComputeFilesToPublish is the target that assembles the publish set from the build output and from every
    referenced project, so removing the .pdb items here covers the referenced projects' symbols as well as
    this one's. It applies to `dotnet publish` alone: `dotnet build` output is untouched, which is what the
    test gates consume.
  -->
  <Target Name="ExcludeSymbolsFromPublishOutput" AfterTargets="ComputeFilesToPublish">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ResolvedFileToPublish
          Remove="@(ResolvedFileToPublish)"
          Condition="'%(Extension)' == '.pdb'" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
